And of course, being a barman, you learn to talk to people.

251
00:20:15,492 --> 00:20:16,353
That was one of my gifts.

252
00:20:17,194 --> 00:20:20,036
So he came over near the bar and I said, what brings you here?

253
00:20:20,896 --> 00:20:23,418
And he looked at me kind of startled and I said, you're Shane.

254
00:20:23,458 --> 00:20:24,019
He said, yeah.

255
00:20:25,460 --> 00:20:31,104
He said he was in a stopover, fog bound, for two days, I think, in Shannon Airport.

256
00:20:32,145 --> 00:20:35,707
It left an enormous impression because here was somebody I admired hugely.

257
00:20:35,847 --> 00:20:37,388
It was like meeting Martin Luther King.

258
00:20:37,929 --> 00:20:40,951
This was a man I looked at and thought, my God, this is the real Martin Luther King.

259
00:20:41,717 --> 00:20:43,177
you know, hero of the revolution.

260
00:20:45,818 --> 00:20:50,159
I did many, many versions of the shade before I came to the final version, and here's a couple

261
00:20:50,179 --> 00:20:50,379
of them.

262
00:20:52,019 --> 00:20:53,739
As you can see, they're overcomplicated.

263
00:20:54,320 --> 00:20:56,520
I need to simplify, so I started simplifying.

264
00:20:57,640 --> 00:21:00,301
These are not in sequence, but you can see what I'm up to.

265
00:21:00,841 --> 00:21:03,701
This was one I was going to do as a poster and didn't,

266
00:21:04,722 --> 00:21:06,562
because that's what he looked like when I actually met him.

267
00:21:13,613 --> 00:21:16,194
Che had been murdered, executed as a prisoner of war.

268
00:21:17,615 --> 00:21:20,236
Then they committed the most obscene act.

269
00:21:21,137 --> 00:21:24,458
They put him on this thing for draining the blood from cattle,

270
00:21:25,779 --> 00:21:26,979
right? And he looked like

271
00:21:27,780 --> 00:21:28,640
the dead Christ.

272
00:21:30,541 --> 00:21:33,682
I was so outraged at the manner of his murder

273
00:21:34,643 --> 00:21:36,784
and the fact that they tried to disappear him,

274
00:21:37,884 --> 00:21:41,446
which has residents in Ireland where we had many disappeared, and we know during the Argentina

275
00:21:41,466 --> 00:21:43,087
Dirty War what disappearance meant.

276
00:21:43,792 --> 00:21:45,633
And I thought, fuck them, that's not gonna

277
00:21:45,673 --> 00:21:51,094
happen.

278
00:21:52,375 --> 00:21:53,775
When I did the Che Guevara image,

279
00:21:54,495 --> 00:21:57,396
I did that deliberately as a piece of propaganda.

280
00:21:57,676 --> 00:22:00,837
I wanted that as simple as possible so anybody could copy it.

281
00:22:01,277 --> 00:22:04,418
I made leaflets, I made prints of it, I gave it to everybody.

282
00:22:05,039 --> 00:22:11,261
I made it free use. I rather grandly stated that it was to be copyright free for all revolutionary

283
00:22:11,281 --> 00:22:12,181
and leftist use.

284
00:22:14,003 --> 00:22:16,624
The Shea image began to proliferate of its own accord.

285
00:22:18,184 --> 00:22:20,305
I've seen it in the most incongruous places.

286
00:22:20,385 --> 00:22:22,246
I've seen it in toilets in Tokyo.

287
00:22:23,626 --> 00:22:25,587
I've seen it endlessly on T-shirts.

288
00:22:28,148 --> 00:22:31,789
It took off and became so world famous, it kind of intimidated me.

289
00:22:32,429 --> 00:22:33,350
It got to the point where I

290
00:22:34,690 --> 00:22:37,391
wouldn't even say I did it because nobody believed you.

291
00:22:41,310 --> 00:22:42,131
I'm very lucky.

292
00:22:42,851 --> 00:22:46,573
Kind of accidentally, I created one of the most iconic images of all time.

293
00:22:47,474 --> 00:22:51,636
And I try not to laugh because it's a serious image, but it feels kind of good.

294
00:22:51,736 --> 00:22:56,059
You know, it's nice at my age to be looking back and thinking, well, I did something decent

295
00:22:56,079 --> 00:22:56,660
with my life.

296
00:22:57,620 --> 00:23:00,482
I commemorated a great man and made him live again.

297
00:23:26,878 --> 00:23:30,259
In the First World War, you have nations fighting in total war.

298
00:23:31,679 --> 00:23:34,640
You don't just have armies, but you have whole populations

299
00:23:35,420 --> 00:23:37,700
pitted against whole populations.

300
00:23:41,501 --> 00:23:46,062
And as a result of that, governments have to justify, really for the first time,

301
00:23:46,822 --> 00:23:50,863
on a major scale, why they are fighting total war.

302
00:23:51,964 --> 00:23:53,824
And that's where propaganda comes in.

303
00:24:11,912 --> 00:24:14,392
Propaganda is used by all the belligerents.

304
00:24:15,153 --> 00:24:19,753
They use the old media, the print, posters, etc.

305
00:24:20,613 --> 00:24:24,934
But they also use the new technologies, and in particular, the cinema.

306
00:24:32,476 --> 00:24:33,176
MUSIC PLAYS

307
00:24:45,191 --> 00:24:46,311
In stark terms,

308
00:24:48,212 --> 00:24:54,254
many of these stories were perceived in the immediate aftermath of World War I to be untrue.

309
00:24:54,654 --> 00:25:01,537
And so propaganda in Britain and America became firmly associated with lies

310
00:25:01,877 --> 00:25:02,497
and falsehood.

311
00:25:03,998 --> 00:25:04,938
Now what's interesting

312
00:25:06,119 --> 00:25:08,059
is that other countries like Germany,

313
00:25:09,380 --> 00:25:09,780
Italy,

314
00:25:11,780 --> 00:25:18,064
Russia, and eventually the Soviet Union, would actually take a different lesson from the

315
00:25:18,305 --> 00:25:19,806
experiences of the First World War.

316
00:25:20,386 --> 00:25:24,629
They wouldn't see propaganda necessarily in these negative terms.

317
00:25:53,938 --> 00:25:57,980
If you look at a film such as The Fall of Berlin, here you see

318
00:25:59,060 --> 00:26:01,721
Starling depicted in cinematic form.

319
00:26:03,782 --> 00:26:08,324
We have an actor who becomes a national treasure who plays Starling in a number of films.

320
00:26:08,464 --> 00:26:09,204
Today, we

321
00:26:09,924 --> 00:26:11,965
celebrate the Elip's birthday.

322
00:26:12,995 --> 00:26:14,416
...over German fascism.

323
00:26:16,417 --> 00:26:21,160
And plays Stalin as this wise, kind of benevolent figure...

324
00:26:21,180 --> 00:26:25,202
...that really sacrifices everything for his country.

325
00:26:25,722 --> 00:26:28,324
And in the fall of Berlin, of course, it's the celebration...

326
00:26:28,344 --> 00:26:33,186
...it's the apotheosis of this wise and benevolent leader.

327
00:26:33,687 --> 00:26:36,448
We love Stalin! Peace

328
00:26:37,469 --> 00:26:38,909
and happiness...

329
00:26:58,736 --> 00:27:03,600
Propaganda is something that we cannot bring our full intelligence to bear on.

330
00:27:04,560 --> 00:27:08,063
So there's something about propaganda that actually disables our intelligence.

331
00:27:09,864 --> 00:27:12,645
And so we might look back and think How could I have believed that?

332
00:27:13,265 --> 00:27:17,428
It's a bit like at the end of a love affair, where you think, how could I possibly have fallen

333
00:27:17,448 --> 00:27:17,689
for that

334
00:27:17,729 --> 00:27:30,178
person?

335
00:27:30,519 --> 00:27:32,040
The disillusionment over Stalin

336
00:27:33,014 --> 00:27:39,039
It seems to me it's very telling here, because what it shows us in retrospect is how much

337
00:27:39,739 --> 00:27:43,042
selective attention was required to go on believing.

338
00:27:43,062 --> 00:27:43,102
In

339
00:27:46,445 --> 00:27:51,188
other words, people had quite actively, if unconsciously, attacked their capacity

340
00:27:52,089 --> 00:27:53,850
for perception and for thought.

341
00:27:54,931 --> 00:27:57,013
It's as though it had never really occurred to them.

342
00:27:59,620 --> 00:28:05,828
And this seemed to me to be an active part of oneself that can determinedly not know what one

343
00:28:05,868 --> 00:28:06,149
knows.

344
00:28:08,993 --> 00:28:12,778
Propaganda is based on how you stop yourself knowing things.

345
00:28:51,614 --> 00:28:53,375
Since we have been talking about propaganda,

346
00:28:54,456 --> 00:28:58,278
I would definitely, definitely, without a doubt, say that a lot

347
00:28:59,655 --> 00:29:01,136
of the

348
00:29:01,856 --> 00:29:05,858
narratives about North Korea that we get in our Western media

349
00:29:06,758 --> 00:29:07,419
are propaganda.

350
00:29:10,900 --> 00:29:13,601
Morten Travik is an artist and entrepreneur

351
00:29:14,302 --> 00:29:16,563
who stages music events around the world.

352
00:29:18,043 --> 00:29:20,404
His special fascination with North Korea

353
00:29:21,165 --> 00:29:26,427
has led him to face the unique challenge of producing concerts in that hermit kingdom.

354
00:29:31,767 --> 00:29:35,210
Many people think that a place like North Korea, it must be like Mordor.

355
00:29:35,230 --> 00:29:37,492
It must be like hell on earth.

356
00:29:37,672 --> 00:29:41,295
And many people make a living out of selling this narrative.

357
00:29:42,516 --> 00:29:46,699
But the fact is, and you can ask anybody who has grown up in a dictatorship

358
00:29:47,480 --> 00:29:48,721
or in a totalitarian system,

359
00:29:50,242 --> 00:29:51,423
people censor themselves.

360
00:29:52,704 --> 00:29:55,707
Because we are comfortable animals, most of us.

361
00:29:56,147 --> 00:29:58,749
We like to stay on the safe side, and we want to keep out of trouble.

362
00:30:01,898 --> 00:30:06,199
Even in North Korea, people actually, many people, know

363
00:30:07,740 --> 00:30:10,501
what is propaganda and what is not propaganda.

364
00:30:12,021 --> 00:30:13,902
And that's a paradox, that people are

365
00:30:14,742 --> 00:30:19,043
living an existence which is so obviously manipulated.

366
00:30:19,643 --> 00:30:21,904
There is a level of total staging

367
00:30:23,064 --> 00:30:24,825
in the average North Korean's life,

368
00:30:26,225 --> 00:30:27,626
but that level is so obvious

369
00:30:28,606 --> 00:30:28,906
that

370
00:30:29,726 --> 00:30:31,127
you can have a parallel existence

371
00:30:32,196 --> 00:30:37,158
below that level of basically a normal life as long as you pay lip service

372
00:30:41,699 --> 00:30:42,379
in any kind of

373
00:30:43,080 --> 00:30:46,881
totalitarian system they say the same that yes we did wave our flags

374
00:30:47,841 --> 00:30:49,682
we went for the may 1st parade

375
00:30:50,422 --> 00:30:51,282
we did all that stuff

376
00:30:52,123 --> 00:30:53,043
we keep our sanity

377
00:31:00,430 --> 00:31:02,752
We, in the more or less free world,

378
00:31:03,913 --> 00:31:09,378
we are so trained now. We are not that susceptible, for instance, to North Korean propaganda.

379
00:31:09,398 --> 00:31:10,639
For us, that is quaint

380
00:31:11,560 --> 00:31:12,781
and kitsch even.

381
00:31:14,062 --> 00:31:14,322
But

382
00:31:15,423 --> 00:31:17,925
we are also subject to

383
00:31:19,026 --> 00:31:23,150
many, many forms of propaganda all the time that are more subtle.

384
00:31:27,293 --> 00:31:30,594
which might lead us to the misconception

385
00:31:31,795 --> 00:31:35,016
that we are somehow less exposed

386
00:31:36,516 --> 00:31:37,717
than the average North Korean.

387
00:31:38,597 --> 00:31:41,258
And this I don't necessarily agree with.

388
00:31:43,839 --> 00:31:46,100
Propaganda is something other people consume.

389
00:31:47,297 --> 00:31:51,319
I mean, I think that that's sort of our default definition, right?

390
00:31:51,459 --> 00:31:56,981
We don't go around, nobody goes around and says, my name is Astra and I love propaganda

391
00:31:57,682 --> 00:32:01,223
because we all like to flatter ourselves and think we don't consume propaganda.

392
00:32:02,864 --> 00:32:03,504
Other people do.

393
00:32:06,026 --> 00:32:09,407
If you look at standard histories of propaganda, what do you see?

394
00:32:09,487 --> 00:32:11,208
You see the Catholic Church.

395
00:32:13,305 --> 00:32:17,107
you see the Soviet Union and Nazi propaganda.

396
00:32:19,969 --> 00:32:23,651
And what's striking to me about these sort of conventional narratives of what propaganda is,

397
00:32:23,711 --> 00:32:25,312
is how much is left out.

398
00:32:26,072 --> 00:32:30,895
Adrienne knows that for some reason she doesn't fit into the picture, but she doesn't know

399
00:32:30,995 --> 00:32:31,395
why.

400
00:32:32,796 --> 00:32:36,438
As a woman in this world, I know there are patriarchal messages, there are sexist messages.

401
00:32:38,209 --> 00:32:42,190
There's no sort of Bureau of Male Affairs pushing those from on high.

402
00:32:42,210 --> 00:32:45,891
They're just sort of in the ether and being transmitted through the culture, and we call them

403
00:32:45,931 --> 00:32:51,192
stereotypes. We talk about bias, but we don't use the term propaganda.

404
00:32:52,412 --> 00:32:54,172
Take a show like The Bachelor.

405
00:32:54,632 --> 00:32:58,433
Lauren B. All these stereotypes are endlessly reinforced.

406
00:33:01,434 --> 00:33:02,954
Lauren, will you accept this rose?

407
00:33:03,614 --> 00:33:07,875
For me, when I look around and I look at American culture, North American culture,

408
00:33:09,077 --> 00:33:13,660
The stuff that strikes me as the most propagandistic is actually the most mainstream culture.

409
00:33:13,720 --> 00:33:15,281
It's the films coming out of Hollywood.

410
00:33:15,701 --> 00:33:17,162
You know, like, She's All That.

411
00:33:18,682 --> 00:33:21,644
Home improvement shows or real estate shows like House Hunters.

412
00:33:22,685 --> 00:33:27,488
It's these things that present a sort of idealized, consumeristic mode of life.

413
00:33:27,828 --> 00:33:30,549
Your grand staircase. How does that make you feel?

414
00:33:31,310 --> 00:33:35,932
Kind of happy inside. The most successful propaganda, I think, ever in the world has been the

415
00:33:35,972 --> 00:33:39,410
propaganda that says, What you really want is to be rich.

416
00:33:41,971 --> 00:33:44,192
Capitalism creates tremendous envy

417
00:33:45,452 --> 00:33:51,035
because a lot of people feel extremely deprived and resentful because they're living in a culture

418
00:33:51,055 --> 00:33:56,737
in which it appears as though 1% of the population is enjoying life as it should be lived

419
00:33:57,997 --> 00:34:00,418
and everybody else is just suffering.

420
00:34:01,339 --> 00:34:04,220
The trouble is we've been sold the wrong picture of a good life.

421
00:34:22,834 --> 00:34:24,195
What do you see your role to be?

422
00:34:25,176 --> 00:34:27,617
I see myself as a political guerrilla artist.

423
00:34:28,378 --> 00:34:30,600
What do you call it? A shit disturber.

424
00:34:31,660 --> 00:34:33,041
I think it's your duty

425
00:34:33,822 --> 00:34:36,224
to disturb shit when you think things are wrong.

426
00:34:46,539 --> 00:34:49,021
I believe something's wrong and it needs to be changed.

427
00:34:49,181 --> 00:34:52,084
And for some strange reason, the smartest among us

428
00:34:52,884 --> 00:34:54,065
don't seem to get it. It's

429
