if they developed an atomic bomb, they could judge its full destructive capacity.

746
01:01:25,349 --> 01:01:30,151
Looking toward Zero Point from the roof of the Red Cross Hospital a mile away, the tremendous

747
01:01:30,211 --> 01:01:33,152
destruction created by the first atomic bomb can be seen.

748
01:01:33,889 --> 01:01:39,093
This foundry, three-tenths of a mile away from Zero Point, shows considerable damage in spite

749
01:01:39,133 --> 01:01:40,494
of fairly good construction.

750
01:01:41,055 --> 01:01:45,178
Always in the back of the mind of the people who were making these considerations were, let's

751
01:01:45,198 --> 01:01:46,920
call them, the propagandistic effects.

752
01:01:47,720 --> 01:01:52,484
It's a message, not only to Japan, but more importantly to the Soviet Union.

753
01:01:54,446 --> 01:01:56,687
Behind the so-called Statue of Liberty,

754
01:01:57,848 --> 01:02:02,372
at the military factories, fenced with barbed wire, guarded by machine guns,

755
01:02:07,299 --> 01:02:11,501
The Cold War is a mobilization of culture and of ideology,

756
01:02:12,502 --> 01:02:14,563
and the stakes of it are global.

757
01:02:15,363 --> 01:02:18,444
Every citizen of the world is potentially a target.

758
01:02:19,545 --> 01:02:24,967
News reporting, for instance, becomes in some sense a form of propaganda too, in that it reports

759
01:02:25,068 --> 01:02:29,190
the kind of virtues of the United States or the victories, for instance, of things like the

760
01:02:29,230 --> 01:02:29,810
space race.

761
01:02:30,776 --> 01:02:34,663
And these become their own form of propaganda as well.

762
01:02:34,724 --> 01:02:39,252
That's one small step for man, one giant leap for mankind.
